---
name: tool_meanings
description: What each tool parameter means to the model.
meanings:
  kind: |-
    Which kind of event this is. Use noted for anything worth writing down that no other kind covers — a move, a new job, finishing a course. A noted event must say what happened in its description.
  description: |-
    What happened, with no names of the people this event links: the person, spouse, child and targets are said by their fields.
  notes: |-
    Free text kept on the event beside its description. Changing it replaces what is there, so read it first and write the whole text.
  end_date: |-
    YYYY-MM-DD. When the event ended, given only when an end date is known; date is when it began.
  location: |-
    Where it happened, when the conversation says where. On a noted event about a move it is the place they moved to.
  item: |-
    On a noted event that records someone's schooling, work, health or where they lived, which of those it is.
  fact: |-
    On a fact question about a person or a couple: the item of the family's basic data it asks about. Name it whenever the question asks for one of the required facts. A question that asks for two items is kept as two questions.
  person: |-
    The person the event is about. On a marriage, bonding, separation or divorce it is one of the two partners and spouse is the other. On a birth or adoption it is a parent, and is left out when the parents are not known — never the same id as child.
  spouse: |-
    The other partner. Required with person on married, bonded, separated and divorced; on a birth it is the second parent.
  child: |-
    For a birth or adoption, who was born or taken in: set child, not person.
  anxiety: |-
    Which way this variable moved, if it moved.
  symptom: |-
    Which way this variable moved, if it moved.
  functioning: |-
    Which way this variable moved, if it moved.
  relationship: |-
    Which relationship value this event carries.
  relationship_targets: |-
    The other people this event's relationship links. Required whenever relationship is set, and never empty.
  relationship_triangles: |-
    A second list of people the relationship links. Required when relationship is inside or outside.
  person_a: |-
    One side of the bond. A bond is between two different people and there is one bond ever between any two of them.
  person_b: |-
    The other side. Leave it out only when nobody named them: the record then adds them as a generically named person, because a bond with one side is not a bond.
  parents: |-
    The id of the pair bond this person was born into. Children are the offspring of a bond, so the two parents must have one first.
  keep: |-
    On merge_people: the person who stays, the one whose record is fuller (named and dated) unless the person you are talking with says otherwise. Your question says which one stays.
  drop: |-
    On merge_people: the person joined into the one kept. Their events, pair-bonds, parent links, questions and impressions go to the kept person and they leave the record, only after the person said yes, they are one.
  take: |-
    On merge_people: for each fact the two records differ on — birth, death, gender, notes, parents — whose is right, keep or drop, as the person said. A fact only one of them has comes over without being named. A differing fact not named here makes the merge refuse, so ask which is right first.
  fields: |-
    What to add to each event: words, what the user said that it came from; notes, in full, since the record only says which events have notes, whenever what was said there matters to the conversation; location, where it happened.
---
